{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-23:5\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The shared ḥ-f-ẓ predicate makes sexual fidelity a plausible part of what the righteous women preserve: 23:5 names guarding the private parts, while al-ghayb in 4:34 remains broader.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"فَٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتُ قَٰنِتَٰتٌ حَٰفِظَٰتٌۭ لِّلْغَيْبِ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:5\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلَّذِينَ هُمْ لِفُرُوجِهِمْ حَٰفِظُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus places ḥāfiẓāt within a marital profile, and 23:5 uses the same guarding root for sexual boundaries.\",\"limits\":\"23:5 identifies private parts as its object; it does not define al-ghayb in 4:34 as sexual fidelity.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:6\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The spouses exception in 23:6 specifies the sexual boundary surrounding the guarding in 23:5, making marital fidelity one possible dimension of 4:34's guarding.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"23:5\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلَّذِينَ هُمْ لِفُرُوجِهِمْ حَٰفِظُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:6\",\"ar\":\"إِلَّا عَلَىٰٓ أَزْوَٰجِهِمْ أَوْ مَا مَلَكَتْ أَيْمَٰنُهُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"حَٰفِظَٰتٌۭ لِّلْغَيْبِ بِمَا حَفِظَ ٱللَّهُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus's righteous women are described through guarding within a marital passage.\",\"limits\":\"The exception defines the boundary in 23:5–6; it does not identify what al-ghayb means in 4:34.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:9\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Preserving prayer in 23:9 sits within a believer profile that also names care for trusts and covenants; this modestly parallels the focus's devotional and guarding qualities.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"23:8\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلَّذِينَ هُمْ لِأَمَٰنَٰتِهِمْ وَعَهْدِهِمْ رَٰعُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:9\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلَّذِينَ هُمْ عَلَىٰ صَلَوَٰتِهِمْ يُحَافِظُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"فَٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتُ قَٰنِتَٰتٌ حَٰفِظَٰتٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus likewise groups devotional and preserving qualities into a profile of righteous women.\",\"limits\":\"23:9 specifies prayer as the object of preservation; it does not explain the focus's guarded domain or marital duties.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:46\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The same ʿ-l-w root describes Pharaoh's people as عَالِينَ and God as عَلِيًّا; this permits a contrast between divine highness and worldly elites' elevation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"23:46\",\"ar\":\"وَكَانُوا۟ قَوْمًا عَالِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ كَانَ عَلِيًّا كَبِيرًا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus closes its household declaration with God's highness, making another Quranic use of the root potentially significant.\",\"limits\":\"23:46 describes Pharaoh's people, not husbands, and does not explicitly contrast their status with the focus's household roles.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:67\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The h-j-r root also appears in 23:67 as تَهْجُرُونَ during an accusation about nighttime talk; this shows that the root alone does not settle the sense of the focus's bed-related command.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"23:67\",\"ar\":\"مُسْتَكْبِرِينَ بِهِۦ سَٰمِرًۭا تَهْجُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"وَٱهْجُرُوهُنَّ فِى ٱلْمَضَاجِعِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus uses the same root in an imperative immediately restricted by a phrase locating the action in beds.\",\"limits\":\"The forms and constructions differ; 23:67 does not determine the meaning of the imperative in 4:34.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:70\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:91\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The exact phrase بَعْضَهُمْ عَلَىٰ بَعْضٍ links God's differentiating some over others in 4:34 with the imagined rivalry in which each deity would rise over another in 23:91. This lets the focus's granted distinction be heard against self-asserted, competing elevation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"بِمَا فَضَّلَ ٱللَّهُ بَعْضَهُمْ عَلَىٰ بَعْضٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:91\",\"ar\":\"وَلَعَلَا بَعْضُهُمْ عَلَىٰ بَعْضٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus grounds a relational distinction in God's act; 23:91 imagines the same some-over-some relation as rivalry among supposed gods.\",\"limits\":\"23:91 is a counterfactual argument against multiple gods, not an explicit commentary on gender or household authority.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:92\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"God is named knower of the unseen and the manifest in 23:92, directly echoing al-ghayb in the focus's description of what the righteous women guard. The pairing can place private conduct under God's knowledge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"حَٰفِظَٰتٌۭ لِّلْغَيْبِ بِمَا حَفِظَ ٱللَّهُ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:92\",\"ar\":\"عَٰلِمِ ٱلْغَيْبِ وَٱلشَّهَٰدَةِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus couples al-ghayb with God's preserving, while 23:92 couples the same noun with God's knowledge.\",\"limits\":\"23:92 does not say that divine knowledge is the reason for the women's guarding or define its particular domain.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-24:31\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus's حَٰفِظَٰتٌۭ لِّلْغَيْبِ has a concrete Quranic parallel in women told to guard their private parts, cover their chests, and not reveal hidden adornment. The same verse also uses the ḍ-r-b root for drawing a covering and striking the feet, with constructions distinct from the focus's command وَٱضْرِبُوهُنَّ.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"حَٰفِظَٰتٌۭ لِّلْغَيْبِ\"},{\"ref\":\"24:31\",\"ar\":\"وَيَحْفَظْنَ فُرُوجَهُنَّ وَلَا يُبْدِينَ زِينَتَهُنَّ\"},{\"ref\":\"24:31\",\"ar\":\"وَلْيَضْرِبْنَ بِخُمُرِهِنَّ عَلَىٰ جُيُوبِهِنَّ\"},{\"ref\":\"24:31\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا يَضْرِبْنَ بِأَرْجُلِهِنَّ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages address women's conduct, guarding, and bodily or private boundaries; 24:31 also supplies distinct ḍ-r-b constructions relevant to the focus's disputed imperative.\",\"limits\":\"The 24:31 forms and objects do not establish the sense of وَٱضْرِبُوهُنَّ, and its rules do not define al-ghayb in 4:34.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-24:32\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"24:32 pairs marriage-making with provision for poverty and names صالحين, while 4:34 names الصالحات and grounds qiwāma partly in spending. Together they place marital roles alongside marriage access and material need.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"بِمَآ أَنفَقُوا۟ مِنْ أَمْوَٰلِهِمْ ۚ فَٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتُ\"},{\"ref\":\"24:32\",\"ar\":\"وَأَنكِحُوا۟ ٱلْأَيَٰمَىٰ مِنكُمْ وَٱلصَّٰلِحِينَ مِنْ عِبَادِكُمْ وَإِمَآئِكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"24:32\",\"ar\":\"إِن يَكُونُوا۟ فُقَرَآءَ يُغْنِهِمُ ٱللَّهُ مِن فَضْلِهِۦ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus joins a righteous-women profile to men's expenditure; 24:32 joins marriage to poverty and divine provision.\",\"limits\":\"The forms صالحات and صالحين differ, and 24:32 does not define the role or rank described in 4:34.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-24:60\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"24:60 gives older women a qualified clothing allowance while restricting display; it is another rule for women's visible conduct, with an age-specific condition absent from the focus.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"24:60\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلْقَوَٰعِدُ مِنَ ٱلنِّسَآءِ ٱلَّٰتِى لَا يَرْجُونَ نِكَاحًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"24:60\",\"ar\":\"أَن يَضَعْنَ ثِيَابَهُنَّ غَيْرَ مُتَبَرِّجَٰتٍۭ بِزِينَةٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"حَٰفِظَٰتٌۭ لِّلْغَيْبِ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages regulate women's conduct and boundaries, though they address different situations.\",\"limits\":\"The clothing allowance concerns older women and public adornment; it does not explain the focus's marital or unseen domain.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:9\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Both verses use سَبِيلًا for a way: 25:9 says the people cannot find one, while 4:34 forbids pursuing one against the women. The contrast displays the noun's range without equating the cases.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"25:9\",\"ar\":\"فَضَلُّوا۟ فَلَا يَسْتَطِيعُونَ سَبِيلًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"فَلَا تَبْغُوا۟ عَلَيْهِنَّ سَبِيلًا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus's closing prohibition makes another Quranic use of the same object noun relevant.\",\"limits\":\"25:9 concerns disbelievers' inability to find a way after being misled, not a legal avenue against women.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:19\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:52\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:67\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The expenditure setting is paired with a q-w-m form in both verses: 4:34 calls men قَوَّٰمُونَ and grounds this partly in spending, while 25:67 praises spending that is قَوَامًا, balanced between excess and stinginess. This raises balance as a possible dimension of financial maintenance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرِّجَالُ قَوَّٰمُونَ عَلَى ٱلنِّسَآءِ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"وَبِمَآ أَنفَقُوا۟ مِنْ أَمْوَٰلِهِمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"25:67\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلَّذِينَ إِذَآ أَنفَقُوا۟ لَمْ يُسْرِفُوا۟ وَلَمْ يَقْتُرُوا۟ وَكَانَ بَيْنَ ذَٰلِكَ قَوَامًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus itself links qiwāma with expenditure; 25:67 links a different q-w-m form with measured expenditure.\",\"limits\":\"قَوَامًا and قَوَّٰمُونَ are distinct forms; 25:67 does not define the focus's role as financial moderation.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:59\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:170\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:3\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:20\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The root غ-ي-ب can denote actual absence: 27:20 asks whether the hoopoe is among the absent. That supports hearing al-ghayb in 4:34 with an absence sense, possibly the spouse's absence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"حَٰفِظَٰتٌۭ لِّلْغَيْبِ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:20\",\"ar\":\"أَمْ كَانَ مِنَ ٱلْغَآئِبِينَ\"}],\"missing\":\"27:20 uses an adjective about an absent bird, not the noun al-ghayb in a marital setting. A Quranic use of that noun for a spouse's absence, or a dictionary account linking the senses, could decide whether this is the focus's intended domain.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:55\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"27:55 explicitly contrasts men and women in a statement about sexual desire, showing the pair used in a distinct Quranic discussion of sexual conduct.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"27:55\",\"ar\":\"أَئِنَّكُمْ لَتَأْتُونَ ٱلرِّجَالَ شَهْوَةًۭ مِّن دُونِ ٱلنِّسَآءِ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرِّجَالُ قَوَّٰمُونَ عَلَى ٱلنِّسَآءِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus also places men and women together in a marital passage, so this gendered sexual contrast is adjacent in subject matter.\",\"limits\":\"27:55 condemns Lot's people's conduct; it does not explain the focus's household arrangement or its commands.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:67\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:18\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Both verses use the kh-w-f root for apprehension: Moses is خَائِفًا while anticipating danger, and the focus addresses men who fear nushuz. The parallel keeps the fear located in the perceiver's outlook.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"28:18\",\"ar\":\"فَأَصْبَحَ فِى ٱلْمَدِينَةِ خَآئِفًۭا يَتَرَقَّبُ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلَّٰتِى تَخَافُونَ نُشُوزَهُنَّ\"}],\"activation\":\"In both passages fear concerns a possible relational or physical danger rather than naming the feared event as already completed.\",\"limits\":\"Moses fears threatened violence in a separate narrative; the shared root does not determine the evidentiary threshold for nushuz.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:25\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:33\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:29\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"29:29 uses ٱلسَّبِيلَ for a path that Lot's people cut, while 4:34 prohibits seeking a سَبِيلًا against women; the shared noun carries a physical or social route alongside a hostile course of action.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:29\",\"ar\":\"وَتَقْطَعُونَ ٱلسَّبِيلَ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"فَلَا تَبْغُوا۟ عَلَيْهِنَّ سَبِيلًا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus's final clause uses the same path noun in an adversative construction.\",\"limits\":\"29:29 does not say the path is against women, and its accusation belongs to a different scene.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:43\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The same Form I root ḍ-r-b appears in نَضْرِبُهَا for setting forth examples, confirming that the root has Quranic uses beyond striking; its object and construction differ from the focus's imperative.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:43\",\"ar\":\"وَتِلْكَ ٱلْأَمْثَٰلُ نَضْرِبُهَا لِلنَّاسِ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"وَٱضْرِبُوهُنَّ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus's third imperative uses ḍ-r-b, whose broad root range is sometimes invoked in its interpretation.\",\"limits\":\"29:43 does not establish which sense the direct-object imperative has in 4:34.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-30:15\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The same ṣ-l-ḥ root appears in the focus's الصالحات and in 30:15's righteous deeds; the latter places that quality in a faith-and-reward profile.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"فَٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتُ\"},{\"ref\":\"30:15\",\"ar\":\"فَأَمَّا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus uses the same root to name the women whose qualities follow.\",\"limits\":\"30:15 describes believers' deeds and reward; it does not explain the domestic sense of الصالحات in 4:34.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-30:21\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"30:21 names tranquility, affection, and mercy as the purpose and texture of spouses' relation. This gives the focus's dispute procedure a positive marital horizon against which rupture and repair can be heard.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"30:21\",\"ar\":\"أَزْوَٰجًۭا لِّتَسْكُنُوٓا۟ إِلَيْهَا وَجَعَلَ بَيْنَكُم مَّوَدَّةًۭ وَرَحْمَةً\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلَّٰتِى تَخَافُونَ نُشُوزَهُنَّ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus moves from a general marital profile to feared discord and a sequence of responses.\",\"limits\":\"30:21 states a broad divine sign; it does not prescribe or interpret the focus's particular procedure.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-30:25\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"A q-w-m form also describes heaven and earth standing by God's command, broadening the root's Quranic range around stability and sustaining order.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"30:25\",\"ar\":\"أَن تَقُومَ ٱلسَّمَآءُ وَٱلْأَرْضُ بِأَمْرِهِۦ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرِّجَالُ قَوَّٰمُونَ عَلَى ٱلنِّسَآءِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus predicates qiwāma of men, making another q-w-m form relevant to its root range.\",\"limits\":\"30:25's intransitive cosmic form is not the focus's intensive form over people and does not define its social meaning.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-30:43\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-31:30\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The same ordered divine epithets recur: al-ʿAliyy al-Kabīr in 31:30 and ʿAliyyan Kabīran in 4:34. This anchors the household coda in a Quranic formula that declares God's unique truth and highness.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"31:30\",\"ar\":\"وَأَنَّ ٱللَّهَ هُوَ ٱلْعَلِىُّ ٱلْكَبِيرُ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ كَانَ عَلِيًّا كَبِيرًا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus ends its account of human relations with the same two divine predicates.\",\"limits\":\"31:30 is a statement about God's truth and the falsehood of what is invoked besides Him; it does not directly gloss the marital commands.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-32:16\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The shared bed noun sits in a strikingly different cluster: believers leave their beds to call on God in fear and spend what they have received, while 4:34 mentions fear, spending, and separation within beds. The parallel makes bed-separation's moral meaning depend on its purpose and setting.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"32:16\",\"ar\":\"تَتَجَافَىٰ جُنُوبُهُمْ عَنِ ٱلْمَضَاجِعِ يَدْعُونَ رَبَّهُمْ خَوْفًۭا وَطَمَعًۭا وَمِمَّا رَزَقْنَٰهُمْ يُنفِقُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"وَبِمَآ أَنفَقُوا۟ مِنْ أَمْوَٰلِهِمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلَّٰتِى تَخَافُونَ نُشُوزَهُنَّ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"وَٱهْجُرُوهُنَّ فِى ٱلْمَضَاجِعِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus brings together the same bed noun, fear, and spending, though with different agents and relations.\",\"limits\":\"32:16 praises night worship and charity; it neither endorses nor explains the marital response in 4:34.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-33:6\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-33:32\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Just before 33:32, the Prophet's wives are described with the verb يَقْنُتْ لِلَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِۦ. This female devotional use of the same q-n-t root supports hearing قَٰنِتَٰتٌ in 4:34 as devotion oriented to God and His messenger, without supplying an unstated object there.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"33:31\",\"ar\":\"وَمَن يَقْنُتْ مِنكُنَّ لِلَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِۦ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"فَٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتُ قَٰنِتَٰتٌ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages describe women's qualities in marital contexts, and 33:31 explicitly gives the devotional verb's object.\",\"limits\":\"33:31 addresses the Prophet's wives and uses a verb; its stated object cannot simply be inserted into the focus's participle.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-33:47\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"33:47 calls God's gift of فضل to believers كبيرًا, bringing together the focus's f-ḍ-l verb and its closing adjective كبيرًا in a divine-giving frame.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"33:47\",\"ar\":\"بِأَنَّ لَهُم مِّنَ ٱللَّهِ فَضْلًۭا كَبِيرًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"بِمَا فَضَّلَ ٱللَّهُ بَعْضَهُمْ عَلَىٰ بَعْضٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ كَانَ عَلِيًّا كَبِيرًا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus joins a divine preference verb to the same adjective used in 33:47 for God's favor.\",\"limits\":\"33:47 concerns believers' promised reward; it does not identify the advantage or its recipients in 4:34.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-33:48\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-33:53\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"33:53 makes household privacy concrete through a screened request and calls that boundary purer for hearts; 33:54 immediately adds that God knows what people reveal or hide. This resonates with the focus's guarding for al-ghayb under God's preservation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"33:53\",\"ar\":\"وَإِذَا سَأَلْتُمُوهُنَّ مَتَٰعًۭا فَسْـَٔلُوهُنَّ مِن وَرَآءِ حِجَابٍۢ ۚ ذَٰلِكُمْ أَطْهَرُ لِقُلُوبِكُمْ وَقُلُوبِهِنَّ\"},{\"ref\":\"33:54\",\"ar\":\"إِن تُبْدُوا۟ شَيْـًٔا أَوْ تُخْفُوهُ فَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ كَانَ بِكُلِّ شَىْءٍ عَلِيمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"حَٰفِظَٰتٌۭ لِّلْغَيْبِ بِمَا حَفِظَ ٱللَّهُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus joins women's guarding of the unseen with God's guarding; the nearby passage joins household privacy with God's knowledge of hidden and manifest acts.\",\"limits\":\"33:53 gives a specific rule for the Prophet's household; the parallel does not make that rule the definition of 4:34.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-33:55\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"33:55 lists family members before whom the Prophet's wives have no offense, showing that Quranic household privacy can be calibrated by relationship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"33:55\",\"ar\":\"لَّا جُنَاحَ عَلَيْهِنَّ فِىٓ ءَابَآئِهِنَّ وَلَآ أَبْنَآئِهِنَّ وَلَآ إِخْوَٰنِهِنَّ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"حَٰفِظَٰتٌۭ لِّلْغَيْبِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus describes women's guarding in a domestic setting, though it does not enumerate observers or exceptions.\",\"limits\":\"33:55 concerns a specific family and a separate privacy rule; it does not specify what the focus's women guard.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"X1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus repeats almost verbatim 4:32's formula that God favored some over others; that earlier verse immediately says men and women each have a share from what they earn. This places the role claim beside a warning against coveting differences and an explicit recognition of both sexes' earnings.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:32\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا تَتَمَنَّوْا۟ مَا فَضَّلَ ٱللَّهُ بِهِۦ بَعْضَكُمْ عَلَىٰ بَعْضٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:32\",\"ar\":\"لِّلرِّجَالِ نَصِيبٌۭ مِّمَّا ٱكْتَسَبُوا۟ ۖ وَلِلنِّسَآءِ نَصِيبٌۭ مِّمَّا ٱكْتَسَبْنَ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"بِمَا فَضَّلَ ٱللَّهُ بَعْضَهُمْ عَلَىٰ بَعْضٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The two verses share the distinctive some-over-some wording, and the earlier verse explicitly distinguishes men's and women's earnings.\",\"limits\":\"4:32 does not identify which differences underlie qiwāma in 4:34 or say the two passages refer to the same advantage.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X2\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The next verse moves from the focus's response to feared marital discord to appointing an arbiter from each spouse's family, with reconciliation as the aim. It presents a wider, bilateral repair frame around the preceding commands.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلَّٰتِى تَخَافُونَ نُشُوزَهُنَّ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:35\",\"ar\":\"وَإِنْ خِفْتُمْ شِقَاقَ بَيْنِهِمَا فَٱبْعَثُوا۟ حَكَمًۭا مِّنْ أَهْلِهِۦ وَحَكَمًۭا مِّنْ أَهْلِهَآ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:35\",\"ar\":\"إِن يُرِيدَآ إِصْلَٰحًۭا يُوَفِّقِ ٱللَّهُ بَيْنَهُمَآ\"}],\"activation\":\"The immediate sequel repeats fear as the trigger but widens the participants to both families and names repair.\",\"limits\":\"4:35 addresses a breach between spouses and does not explicitly state when its arbitration stage follows the commands in 4:34.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X3\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus's ban on seeking a way against women echoes the surah's formula for people who withdraw from fighting and offer peace: God has made no way for the believers against them. 4:141 likewise denies disbelievers a way over believers. These uses make the focus's final clause sound like a boundary on adversarial leverage.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"فَإِنْ أَطَعْنَكُمْ فَلَا تَبْغُوا۟ عَلَيْهِنَّ سَبِيلًا\"},{\"ref\":\"4:90\",\"ar\":\"فَمَا جَعَلَ ٱللَّهُ لَكُمْ عَلَيْهِمْ سَبِيلًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"4:141\",\"ar\":\"وَلَن يَجْعَلَ ٱللَّهُ لِلْكَٰفِرِينَ عَلَى ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ سَبِيلًا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus and 4:90 share the construction of no way against a group; 4:141 uses the same noun to deny hostile dominion.\",\"limits\":\"The other passages concern peace and the relation between disbelievers and believers; they do not equate those groups with women in the focus.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X4\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"4:128 uses the same nushūz noun when a wife fears it from her husband, then names settlement between the spouses as an available response. Nushūz is therefore not a fixed female identity in this surah.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلَّٰتِى تَخَافُونَ نُشُوزَهُنَّ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:128\",\"ar\":\"وَإِنِ ٱمْرَأَةٌ خَافَتْ مِنۢ بَعْلِهَا نُشُوزًا أَوْ إِعْرَاضًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"4:128\",\"ar\":\"فَلَا جُنَاحَ عَلَيْهِمَآ أَن يُصْلِحَا بَيْنَهُمَا صُلْحًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The same surah reuses nushūz with reversed spouses and a repair response.\",\"limits\":\"4:128 has its own circumstances and settlement procedure; it does not replace the particular directions in 4:34.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X5\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The surah uses q-w-m language for obligations tied to justice: it tells believers to be قَوَّٰمِينَ بِٱلْقِسْطِ, and elsewhere speaks of standing for orphans with justice. This supplies an ethical measure for hearing the focus's قَوَّٰمُونَ عَلَى ٱلنِّسَاءِ.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرِّجَالُ قَوَّٰمُونَ عَلَى ٱلنِّسَآءِ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:127\",\"ar\":\"وَأَن تَقُومُوا۟ لِلْيَتَٰمَىٰ بِٱلْقِسْطِ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:135\",\"ar\":\"كُونُوا۟ قَوَّٰمِينَ بِٱلْقِسْطِ شُهَدَآءَ لِلَّهِ وَلَوْ عَلَىٰٓ أَنفُسِكُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus uses the same q-w-m root for a relational role; nearby surah passages explicitly attach standing language to justice, even against oneself and close relatives.\",\"limits\":\"4:127 and 4:135 address justice toward orphans and truthful testimony, not marital authority directly.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X6\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"4:19 places the focus's household procedure within a wider command to treat women with kindness and a ban on constraining them to recover what was given. That context limits using household leverage for financial gain or mistreatment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:19\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا تَعْضُلُوهُنَّ لِتَذْهَبُوا۟ بِبَعْضِ مَآ ءَاتَيْتُمُوهُنَّ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:19\",\"ar\":\"وَعَاشِرُوهُنَّ بِٱلْمَعْرُوفِ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"وَبِمَآ أَنفَقُوا۟ مِنْ أَمْوَٰلِهِمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"فَلَا تَبْغُوا۟ عَلَيْهِنَّ سَبِيلًا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages regulate treatment of women in marriage; the focus itself joins men's expenditure to a later prohibition on pursuing a way against them.\",\"limits\":\"4:19 addresses inheritance-like constraint and marital kindness; it does not define the specific responses listed in 4:34.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X7\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The earlier legal sequence in 4:15–16 also joins restriction to an exit: women are held in houses until death or God makes a way, and the people addressed must turn aside if the pair repent and reform. This links the focus's conditional stop to a broader limit on continuing corrective action.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:15\",\"ar\":\"فَأَمْسِكُوهُنَّ فِى ٱلْبُيُوتِ حَتَّىٰ يَتَوَفَّىٰهُنَّ ٱلْمَوْتُ أَوْ يَجْعَلَ ٱللَّهُ لَهُنَّ سَبِيلًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"4:16\",\"ar\":\"فَإِن تَابَا وَأَصْلَحَا فَأَعْرِضُوا۟ عَنْهُمَا\"},{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"فَإِنْ أَطَعْنَكُمْ فَلَا تَبْغُوا۟ عَلَيْهِنَّ سَبِيلًا\"}],\"activation\":\"The passages share women's legal treatment, a conditional response, and the s-b-l vocabulary of a way or avenue.\",\"limits\":\"The earlier verses concern a different offense and response; they establish a broader pattern, not equivalence between the rulings.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X8\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"30:26 says all in the heavens and earth are قَٰنِتُونَ to God. This parallel supports reading the focus's قَٰنِتَٰتٌ as a devotional quality whose ultimate orientation is divine, without making its unstated object explicit.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:34\",\"ar\":\"فَٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتُ قَٰنِتَٰتٌ\"},{\"ref\":\"30:26\",\"ar\":\"وَلَهُۥ مَن فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ ۖ كُلٌّۭ لَّهُۥ قَٰنِتُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus uses the q-n-t root in its profile of righteous women; 30:26 applies the root to all creation in relation to God.\",\"limits\":\"30:26 uses a masculine plural participle for creation and does not specify the focus's intended human object or social application.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
